    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "From where do contemporary prototyping and testing practices arise? How does biased data\r\ninfluence prototype creation? The industrial designer Henry Dreyfuss is often credited within interaction\r\ndesign and human-computer interaction communities as an important figure in the movement towards\r\nuser-testing. Dreyfuss’ methodology advocates for including the end-user in early stages of the design\r\nprocess. This shift within the history of industrial design was significant as it broke with the tradition of a\r\nwaterfall-type of approach by incorporating user feedback into product development. Dreyfuss’\r\nprototypes of the “average” American user, titled “Joe and Josephine,” influenced many designs for\r\ndecades after their dissemination. However, Dreyfuss’ data source for the creation of these designs has\r\nnot been examined closely. In crafting “average” bodies, Dreyfuss reflected, in his choice of data set,\r\nsocio-cultural specific notions of “normal.” This essay looks to re-examine the historical foundations for\r\ncontemporary user-testing practices in an effort to shed light on issues of biased data within\r\ntechnological design processes.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Caitlin Cary Burke (March 2022). “Henry Dreyfuss, User Personas, and the Cybernetic Body.” Interfaces: Essays and Reviews in Computing and Culture Vol. 3, Charles Babbage Institute, University of Minnesota, 32-44.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
